---
import Article from "../../layouts/Article.astro";
import { articles } from "../../data/insights.js";
const article = articles.find((a) => a.slug === "michigan-flow-through-entity-tax-s-corp-owners");
---
<Article article={article}>

<p>
  Michigan's flow-through entity tax has been available since 2021, and for its first three
  years it carried a design flaw that made it genuinely hard to use well: you had to commit
  before you knew whether it would help. That changed, and a lot of advice still online has
  not caught up.
</p>

<h2>What the election does</h2>

<p>
  Partnerships and S corporations can elect to pay Michigan income tax at the entity level
  rather than passing the full liability through to owners. The entity pays at the individual
  income tax rate, and each owner receives a refundable credit for their share.
</p>
<p>
  The point is federal, not state. State income tax paid personally is an itemized deduction
  subject to the federal SALT cap. State income tax paid by the business is an ordinary
  business deduction, which reduces the income flowing through on the K-1 before the cap ever
  applies. The IRS blessed this structure in Notice 2020-75, and most states with an income
  tax now offer some version.
</p>
<p>
  The saving is not the state tax — that is owed either way. It is the federal tax on the
  deduction you would otherwise have lost.
</p>

<h2>What changed, and why it matters</h2>

<p>
  For tax years 2021 through 2023, the election had to be made by the fifteenth day of the
  third month <em>of</em> the tax year — March 15 for calendar-year filers. That meant
  committing to an irrevocable three-year election in the first quarter, before you knew what
  the year's profit would be, whether an owner would leave, or whether anyone's federal
  position would make the election worthwhile.
</p>
<p>
  Michigan fixed this. Legislation signed in January 2025 moved the deadline for tax years
  beginning on or after January 1, 2024 to the last day of the ninth month after the end of
  the tax year — September 30 for calendar-year filers.
</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>What that buys you</strong>
  <p>
    You can now decide with the full year's numbers in front of you, after the K-1s are
    drafted and each owner's federal picture is known. The decision moved from a forecast to
    an arithmetic problem.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>Three things the extension did not change</h2>

<h3>It is still irrevocable for three years</h3>
<p>
  The election binds the entity for the year of election and the two following years. An
  owner group that changes composition, a business that swings from profit to loss, or an
  owner who moves to a state with a different credit regime all have to live inside that
  window. This is the part that deserves modelling, and it is the reason the extension is
  helpful rather than sufficient.
</p>

<h3>Electing late has a cash cost</h3>
<p>
  If you elect after the annual return due date, the full liability for that year is due on
  the day you elect. For a profitable entity that can be a substantial single payment with no
  notice. The extension gives you information; it does not give you time to fund the decision.
</p>

<h3>Timing still governs the federal deduction</h3>
<p>
  A cash-basis entity generally deducts the tax in the year it is paid. Electing in September
  for the prior year means the payment lands in the current year, which moves the federal
  deduction with it. That is not necessarily bad, but it is a different year's return and it
  needs to be modelled rather than assumed.
</p>

<h2>Who it still helps, now that the SALT cap has moved</h2>

<p>
  The 2025 federal legislation raised the SALT cap substantially above the original $10,000,
  with a phase-down at higher incomes and a scheduled reversion later this decade. That
  changed the arithmetic without changing the conclusion for most owners of profitable
  Michigan entities.
</p>
<p>
  The election tends to help when:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Owners already exhaust the cap on property taxes and other state taxes before any business income tax is counted</li>
  <li>Owner income is high enough that the cap phases down</li>
  <li>The entity is meaningfully profitable, so the entity-level tax is large relative to the cost of compliance</li>
  <li>Owners are Michigan residents who will actually use the refundable credit</li>
</ul>
<p>
  It tends to help less, or not at all, when the entity is marginally profitable, when owners
  are non-residents whose home states treat the credit unfavorably, or when the owner group
  is likely to change inside the three-year window.
</p>

<h2>The mechanics</h2>

<ul>
  <li>The election is made by submitting a payment through Michigan Treasury Online directed to the elected tax year. There is no form and no late-election relief.</li>
  <li>The annual FTE return is due the last day of the third month after year end.</li>
  <li>Estimated payments follow the ordinary quarterly schedule.</li>
  <li>Owners claim the refundable credit on their individual returns; the entity reports each owner's share.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What we would actually do</h2>

<p>
  Model it per owner rather than for the entity. The election is made at the entity level but
  the benefit is realized on individual returns, and it is common for an election to help
  three owners and be neutral or negative for a fourth. That conversation is easier in
  August with real numbers than in March with a forecast.
</p>
<p>
  Then check whether the three-year commitment survives your own plans. If a partner is
  retiring, an owner is relocating, or a sale is plausible inside the window, the election is
  a constraint as much as a benefit.
</p>

</Article>
